Prim’s and Kruskal’s Algorithms for Minimum Spanning Tree
1. Aim
To implement Prim’s Algorithm and Kruskal’s Algorithm to find the Minimum Spanning Tree (MST) of a weighted, undirected graph representing internet cable connections between municipal corporation offices, minimizing the total installation cost.
2. Problem Statement
A city municipal corporation plans to install internet cable connections between different offices. The cost of connecting each pair of offices varies depending on distance and installation expenses. Implement Prim’s and Kruskal’s Algorithms to determine the MST so that all offices remain connected with minimum total installation cost.
3. Objectives
To understand the concept of a Minimum Spanning Tree.
To implement Prim’s Algorithm.
To implement Kruskal’s Algorithm.
To calculate the minimum total cost of connecting all offices.
To compare the working of both algorithms.
4. Theory
4.1 Graph
A graph is a non-linear data structure consisting of vertices and edges. A vertex represents a municipal office, an edge represents a possible cable connection, and the edge weight represents installation cost. A weighted, undirected graph models this network.
4.2 Spanning Tree
A spanning tree connects all vertices of a connected graph without forming any cycle. Its properties are:
It contains all vertices of the original graph.
It contains exactly V − 1 edges, where V is the number of vertices.
It does not contain any cycle.
There is exactly one path between every pair of vertices in the tree.
4.3 Minimum Spanning Tree (MST)
A Minimum Spanning Tree is a spanning tree whose total edge weight is minimum among all possible spanning trees of a connected, weighted, undirected graph.
MST Cost = sum of the weights of the selected V − 1 edges.
Applications include internet cable installation, telephone networks, electrical distribution, road planning, and water pipeline networks.
5. Example Network
Consider five municipal offices A, B, C, D, and E. The possible cable connections and their installation costs are shown below. The costs are illustrative units.
Connection	Installation Cost
A – B	2
A – C	3
B – C	1
B – D	4
C – D	5
